  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0159 database contains nine tables that represent various entities and relationships in a film rental store. The tables are:\n1. actor: This table contains information about the actors who have appeared in films. Each actor has an actor ID, first name, last name, address ID, and a timestamp for the last update.\n2. address: This table contains information about the addresses of the actors and the rental store. Each address has an address ID, address, address2, district, city ID, postal code, phone number, and a timestamp for the last update.\n3. category: This table contains information about the categories of films. Each category has a category ID, name, and a timestamp for the last update.\n4. city: This table contains information about the cities where the rental store is located. Each city has a city ID, city name, country ID, and a timestamp for the last update.\n5. country: This table contains information about the countries where the rental store is located. Each country has a country ID, country name, and a timestamp for the last update.\n6. film: This table contains information about the films that are available for rent. Each film has a film ID, title, description, release year, language ID, original language ID, rental duration, rental rate, length, replacement cost, rating, special features, and a timestamp for the last update.\n7. film_actor: This table contains information about the actors who have appeared in each film. Each film-actor combination has an actor ID, film ID, and a timestamp for the last update.\n8. film_category: This table contains information about the categories of films. Each film-category combination has a film ID, category ID, and a timestamp for the last update.\n9. language: This table contains information about the languages in which films are available. Each language has a language ID, name, and a timestamp for the last update.\nThe tables are related to each other through foreign keys, which ensure that the data is consistent and accurate. For example, the film table has a foreign key to the language table, which ensures that the language of the film is valid. The film_actor table has foreign keys to the actor and film tables, which ensures that the actors who have appeared in each film are valid. The film_category table has foreign keys to the film and category tables, which ensures that the categories of each film are valid.\nOverall, the schemabench_0159 database provides a comprehensive and accurate representation of the data in a film rental store, with clear relationships between the different tables and a consistent structure.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context\n\nThis project involves the development of a Film Industry module within the LCO Data Explorer, a web application designed to facilitate the exploration and analysis of film-related data. The system stores detailed records of films, actors, filming locations, languages, and film categories, enabling users to perform robust data queries and manipulations for research and real-world applications.\n\n2) Objectives\n\n- To provide a comprehensive database that supports the efficient storage and retrieval of film industry data.\n- To enable users to conduct detailed analytics and case studies on films and their related entities.\n- To ensure data consistency and ease of update across all entities.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Researchers: Require access to detailed film data for academic and industry research.\n- Film Industry Analysts: Utilize data for trend analysis and market insights.\n- System Administrators: Handle the maintenance and update of the database to ensure data integrity.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions\n\n- Users should be able to query films by title, description, release year, and ratings.\n- The system should allow searching for actors by name and associated films.\n- The module must support queries for locations including city and country of filming.\n- Users need the ability to categorize films and search based on language or category.\n- The system should handle updates to actors, films, locations, languages, and categories seamlessly.\n\n5) Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- The system must ensure high availability and data consistency through robust timestamp management.\n- Database performance should support complex queries and manipulate large datasets efficiently.\n- The solution should scale with increasing data and user demand, maintaining optimal performance.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_codellama13b/0159.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0159\nCREATE TABLE `actor` (\n  `actor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `first_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`actor_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `actor_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`address_id`) REFERENCES `address` (`address_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `address` (\n  `address_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `address2` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `district` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `city_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `postal_code` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `phone` varchar(25) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`address_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `address_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`city_id`) REFERENCES `city` (`city_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `category` (\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`category_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `city` (\n  `city_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `country_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`city_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `city_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`country_id`) REFERENCES `country` (`country_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `country` (\n  `country_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `country` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`country_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film` (\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `title` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `release_year` year DEFAULT NULL,\n  `language_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `original_language_id` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `rental_duration` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `rental_rate` decimal(5,2) NOT NULL,\n  `length` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `replacement_cost` decimal(5,2) NOT NULL,\n  `rating` varchar(10) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `special_features` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`film_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `film_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`language_id`) REFERENCES `language` (`language_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `film_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`original_language_id`) REFERENCES `language` (`language_id`) ON DELETE SET NULL ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film_actor` (\n  `actor_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`actor_id`, `film_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `film_actor_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`actor_id`) REFERENCES `actor` (`actor_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `film_actor_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`film_id`) REFERENCES `film` (`film_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `film_category` (\n  `film_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `category_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`film_id`, `category_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `film_category_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`film_id`) REFERENCES `film` (`film_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `film_category_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`category_id`) REFERENCES `category` (`category_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `language` (\n  `language_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `last_update` timestamp NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`language_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
